.Dd $Mdocdate: September 22 2026 $
.Dt FUGUPASS-REPL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fugupass-repl
.Nd FuguPass interface process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the interface process of a FuguPass interactive session.
.Xr fugupass 1
spawns it, and no other run of it works.
It takes no option and no argument.
.Pp
The core process holds the vault, and
.Nm
holds the prompt.
It reads one operator command line, it sends that line to the core
process, and it shows each reply line of the core.
It holds no secret.
The core process reads the passphrase from the terminal, and it
prints each secret to the terminal.
.Pp
The core process spawns
.Nm
with two pipes: the request pipe on file descriptor 3, and the reply
pipe on file descriptor 4.
An absent descriptor gives a report on the standard error and the
exit status 1.
.Pp
Each line of the two pipes ends with one newline.
A request line holds one operator command line, without the space at
each end, and an empty command line sends no request.
The first byte of a reply line is the tag.
The tag
.Sq >
leads one line of the command output, and the tag
.Sq =
leads the end line of one reply.
The rest of the end line is
.Dq ok
or
.Dq fail .
The tag keeps the frame apart from the text, so an entry name cannot
forge an end line.
A line with another tag is a protocol failure, and
.Nm
stops.
.Pp
One process owns the terminal at a time:
.Nm
at the prompt, and the core process while a command runs.
.Nm
gives the terminal settings back before each request, and on every
exit path.
.Pp
The prompt takes the six session commands of
.Xr fugupass 1 ,
and the core process runs each one.
The prompt also takes the two commands below, and they reach no core
path:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm help
Show one line of each command, with the summary of that command.
.It Cm quit
End the session.
The core process then locks the vault, and it erases every session
secret.
.El
.Pp
The line editor gives an emacs-style key subset.
.Ic Tab
completes the word before the cursor, and it cycles through the
candidates.
The candidates of the first word are the command names, and the
candidates of every later word are the entry names of the open index.
.Nm
takes those names from the reply of one
.Cm ls
request.
It sends that request before the first prompt, and it shows no line
of that reply.
Each
.Cm ls
of the operator refreshes the same set.
.Pp
The history holds the command lines of this session, in memory alone.
.Nm
writes no history file, because a history file names the entries of
the vault.
.Pp
Each line of the core process passes the display filter before it
reaches the terminal.
The filter keeps printable ASCII, the newline and the tab.
It replaces every other byte with a question mark, and it removes
.Dv DEL
and the C1 range.
It breaks no UTF-8 sequence.
An entry name and the error text of an oracle carry external bytes,
and an escape sequence of such bytes can rewrite what the operator
sees.
.Pp
Without a terminal on the standard input,
.Nm
reads plain lines.
It then gives no line editing, no history recall, and no escape
output, so a scripted test drives a session in that mode.
.Pp
.Nm
pledges
.Dq stdio tty
after it loads its modules, so it opens no file, it creates no
process, and it reaches no network.
On a system that is not
.Ox ,
the pledge restricts nothing.
.Pp
The session ends on
.Cm quit ,
at the end of the operator input, on an interrupt, and at the closed
reply pipe of a core process that locked the vault.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The
.Nm
utility exits 0 at the end of a session, and 1 on a failure.
The failures are an absent pipe, an argument on the command line, and
a reply line with an unknown tag.
Every report goes to the standard error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fugupass 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
